Unreleased (0.10.0):

- incompatible changes:
  - spy and spyc are now at bin/spy and bin/spyc
  - -M removed: an imported standard module is registered and linked;
    -D sys names one only a dynamic import reaches
- spyc finds C modules, NAME.c and libNAME.a, in the search roots
- spyc run, spyc test, spyc clean and spyc version; spyc build is spyc;
  run and test cache programs in __pycache__/spyc
- spyc is a Python program on the engine, built by the previous release's
  spyc; make downloads it (SPYC_BOOTSTRAP names another)
- spyc programs write sys.stderr to stderr
- spyc -D PKG.* adds the modules in name order
- the unicode_escape codec
- a generator that dies while its frame lives on is gone to weak references
- the asend, athrow and anext awaitable types define their methods, so
  they are awaitable to type-based checks
- a union keeps unhashable members apart: they are compared by equality
  only with each other, and make the union unhashable
- a collection due inside __del__ waits for it, so it frees what it finds
- co_names of module and class code lists their names, all in first-use order
- a collection clears weak references with a callback before it runs
  finalizers, so no callback sees a resurrected object
- reversed() of a memoryview, or of a class whose metaclass defines
  __getitem__, reads its items through __getitem__
- memoryview: a view released by a callback is never read or written again;
  '?' items of any nonzero byte are true; memoryviews are weakly referenceable
- FrameLocalsProxy follows CPython for non-str keys, reversed(), construction
  and mapping patterns; locals() includes the keys stored through it
- deleting f_lineno or f_trace_opcodes raises AttributeError
- iter() calls a Python __iter__ without nesting on the C stack, so it can pause
- lists made of an instance's items, and bytes(), ask its length hint after
  __iter__, as 3.15 does; list(), sorted() and [*x] can pause in it
- str.join, bytes.join and dict() pairs list an iterator as list() does,
  calling its __iter__ again and asking its length hint; dict() pairs call __iter__ once
- and and or call a Python __bool__ or __len__ without nesting on the C stack, so they can pause
- set(), frozenset() and {*x} call a Python __hash__ without nesting on the C stack, so they can pause
- any() and all() call a Python __bool__ or __len__ without nesting on the C stack, so they can pause
- map() of str, repr and the other conversions, and filter() of None or bool, call
  an item's Python method without nesting on the C stack, so they can pause
- str % values calls a Python __str__ or __repr__, also of a list's or tuple's items, without
  nesting on the C stack, so it can pause
- repr(), str(), print() and f-strings call the Python __repr__ of items in a list or tuple
  nested in a list or tuple without nesting on the C stack, so they can pause
- repr(), str(), print(), f-strings and str % call the Python __repr__ of a dict's keys and values
  and a set's items without nesting on the C stack, so they can pause
- dict.fromkeys() calls a key's Python __hash__ without nesting on the C stack, so it can pause
- == and != of two dicts, also nested in lists, tuples or dicts or holding them, call their
  values' Python __eq__ without nesting on the C stack, so they can pause
- list.index(), count() and remove() and tuple.index() and count() of an item a call gives call
  a Python __eq__ without nesting on the C stack, so they can pause
- sorted() of a set, a dict, its keys or a list iterator calls a Python __lt__ without nesting
  on the C stack, so it can pause
- zip(), enumerate() and map() of a map take its items without nesting on the C stack, so a
  Python function or conversion it calls can pause
- dict() and dict.update() of pairs, also from zip() or a generator, and set() of zip() call a
  key's Python __hash__ and __eq__ without nesting on the C stack, so they can pause
- set.update() calls an element's Python __hash__ without nesting on the C stack, so it can pause
- set(), frozenset(), set.update() and dict.fromkeys() call the Python __eq__ of a key
  of the same hash without nesting on the C stack, so they can pause
- x < y, where y's class subclasses x's and inherits object's reflected method, calls x's
  Python comparison method without nesting on the C stack, so it can pause
- Reading a data descriptor that an instance's own entry shadows calls its Python __get__
  without nesting on the C stack, so it can pause
- next() of an instance, also with a default, calls its Python __next__ without nesting on the
  C stack, so it can pause, and runs a __next__ that calls nothing 2.2x faster
- enumerate(), zip() and map() of an instance, and next() of them, call its Python __next__
  without nesting on the C stack, so they can pause
- zip(), map(), enumerate() and filter() call an instance's Python __iter__ without nesting on
  the C stack, so they can pause
- == and != of two dicts call their keys' Python __eq__ without nesting on the C stack, so they
  can pause
- an attribute reference calls a Python __getattribute__ and then the __getattr__ its
  AttributeError reaches without nesting on the C stack, so it can pause
- reversed() of a sequence calls its Python __len__ without nesting on the C stack, so it can pause
- list(), sorted(), list.extend(), +=, starred displays and bytes() of an iterator or reversed
  iterator over a sequence ask its length hint, calling the sequence's __len__
- next() of an iterator over a sequence, a map or a filter calls its Python __getitem__ or function
  without nesting on the C stack, so it can pause
- an await of an async generator's asend() or __anext__(), and an async for over one, resume its
  body without nesting on the C stack, so it can pause
- an unreachable async generator suspended at a yield closes without nesting on the C stack, so
  its cleanup can pause
- a starred iterator over a sequence, map, filter, zip or enumerate in a display or a call takes
  its items without nesting on the C stack, so it can pause
- a dict display's ** item calls a mapping's Python keys() and __getitem__ without nesting on the
  C stack, so it can pause
- reading a class's attribute, also by getattr() or hasattr(), calls a descriptor's Python __get__
  or a metaclass property's getter without nesting on the C stack, so it can pause
- an attribute read calls a Python __getattr__ after a descriptor's or __getattribute__'s
  AttributeError, and a metaclass's __getattr__ or __getattribute__, without nesting on the C stack
- a coroutine's __await__ wrapper resumes the coroutine, and an unreachable coroutine suspended in
  an await closes, without nesting on the C stack, so they can pause
- an unreachable generator or coroutine that ignores its close in an except block is still
  reclaimed, with its locals and the iterator it waits on
- map() of a class calls its Python __init__ without nesting on the C stack
- x in a list or tuple, x a tuple, list or dict whose items have a Python __eq__, does not nest on
  the C stack
- any(), all(), str(), repr() and format() called with starred arguments do not nest on the C stack
- type() and type.__new__() call a Python __set_name__ and __init_subclass__ without nesting on the C stack
- a super object finds a base's __get__, __self__ or __thisclass__ before its own
- an instance of a property subclass calls its Python getter without nesting on the C stack
- iter(f, sentinel) calls a Python f without nesting on the C stack
- a method call whose attribute a Python getter or __getattr__ reads calls both without nesting on the C stack
- zip, map and enumerate of iter(f, sentinel) call a Python f without nesting on the C stack
- a comprehension calls its first iterable's Python __iter__ without nesting on the C stack
- dir() calls a Python __dir__, and int() and float() a Python __index__, without nesting on the C stack
- str % calls an instance's Python __int__, __index__ and __float__ without nesting on the C stack
- str.format's !r, !s and !a call an instance's Python __repr__ and __str__ without nesting on the C stack
- ascii() calls a Python __repr__, and round() an ndigits' Python __index__, without nesting on the C stack
- raise of a class, or from a cause class, calls its Python __new__ without nesting on the C stack
- dict() and dict.update() take the pairs a Python __iter__ gives without nesting on the C stack
- a class body binds its implicit names through its namespace's Python __setitem__ and __getitem__ without
  nesting on the C stack
- from __future__ import annotations keeps an __annotations__ the namespace has; any annotated target sets it up
- a class body binds __annotate_func__ after its statements, and a module __annotate__ before
  __conditional_annotations__
- every module annotation is conditional: a module stopped part way reports only those it ran
- a generator's throw() of a class calls its Python __new__ without nesting on the C stack
- throw() of a class whose call makes an exception of another class throws the class called with it
- print() calls a file's Python write and flush without nesting on the C stack, looking write up for each
  write; a str subclass sep or end is accepted
- raise of a class whose call returns an exception of another class raises the class called with it,
  noting a failure ("Normalization failed")
- a memoryview's item, and a store into a bytearray or memoryview, call a Python __index__ without nesting
  on the C stack; a multi-dimensional memoryview's item converts its index before it fails
- print(*objects) calls their Python __str__ without nesting on the C stack
- property.__get__() of an object calls a Python getter without nesting on the C stack
- a generator's throw() of a class, a value and a None traceback calls its Python __init__ without nesting
  on the C stack
- dict() and dict.update() with keywords take a Python __iter__'s pairs or keys() and __getitem__'s
  items without nesting on the C stack
- raise calls a class again with an exception of another class its Python __new__ made, running its
  __new__ and __init__ without nesting on the C stack
- a class body's def, class, annotated assignment and del of a name call its namespace's Python
  __setitem__ and __delitem__ without nesting on the C stack
- bytes % calls an instance's Python __int__, __index__, __float__, __repr__ and __bytes__ without nesting on
  the C stack; its %s of a bytes subclass takes its bytes, and a str's %c takes __index__'s TypeError as its own
- str.format's conversion before a nested spec calls a Python __repr__ or __str__ without nesting on the C stack
- str.format of a list, tuple, dict, set or frozenset field calls its items' Python __repr__ without nesting on
  the C stack
- a multi-dimensional memoryview's item at a tuple key calls its items' Python __index__ without nesting on
  the C stack
- bytearray % calls an instance's Python methods without nesting on the C stack, the format exported meanwhile
- str.format of a container field with a nested spec calls its items' Python __repr__ without nesting on the C stack
- raise of an exception class, or from a cause class, calls its metaclass's Python __call__ without nesting on the C stack
- super().__call__ in a metaclass's __call__ calls the class's Python __new__ and __init__ without nesting on the C stack
- map() of a class, and a method call of one a Python getter reads, call its Python __new__ without nesting on the C stack
- a class body loads names through its namespace's Python __getitem__ without nesting on the C stack
- type.__call__(cls, ...) called unbound runs the class's Python __new__ and __init__ without nesting on the C stack
- generator.throw(type, value, traceback) resumes the generator without nesting on the C stack
- a type statement and a generic def or class make their type parameters, generic base and alias without nesting
  on the C stack; a type alias's repr is its __qualname__
- dir() lists and sorts the generator a Python __dir__ makes or returns without nesting on the C stack
- an if or a chain tests a comparison's result by its Python __bool__ or __len__ without nesting on the C stack
- isinstance() and issubclass() of a nested tuple, and the truth of a check's result, call Python without nesting
  on the C stack
- exec() and eval() code loads names through a locals mapping's Python __getitem__ without nesting on the C stack
- a list's slice store of a generator or a Python iterator takes its items without nesting on the C stack
- sorted(), list.sort(), min() and max() by a builtin key such as len call an item's Python method without nesting
  on the C stack
- filter() of a builtin function such as len calls an item's Python method without nesting on the C stack
- so do sorted(), min() and max() by a builtin key over a list or tuple iterator, a set or a dict
- iter(f, sentinel), also in a zip, map or enumerate, compares with a Python __eq__ of its sentinel without
  nesting on the C stack
- a list's slice store or deletion calls its parts' Python __index__, then takes a generator's items, without
  nesting on the C stack
- a list's += of a generator or a Python iterator takes its items without nesting on the C stack
- super() reads a base's property or descriptor through its Python getter or __get__ without nesting on
  the C stack, also in getattr() and hasattr()
- a loop or consumer resumes a generator in a yield from of a builtin iterator, or of a map of a Python
  function, without nesting on the C stack
- resuming a generator in a yield from of an instance after a yield statement no longer crashes
- throw() and close() of a generator in a yield from of a builtin iterator do not nest on the C stack
- throw() of a class into a yield from of an instance calls its Python throw without nesting on the C stack
- a match statement's patterns call a Python __eq__, __len__, get, __instancecheck__ or getter without nesting
  on the C stack
- a comparison with an instance of a builtin type's subclass calls its reflected Python method without nesting
  on the C stack
- a dict or set key of a builtin type's subclass calls its Python __hash__ without nesting on the C stack
- a module's Python __getattr__ runs without nesting on the C stack, after the module type's attributes,
  such as object's __repr__, __eq__ and __init__, which a module now has
- reading __annotations__ calls the compiler's or a Python __annotate__ without nesting on the C stack
- a dict or set lookup calls the Python __eq__ of a builtin type's subclass key without nesting on the C stack
- filter() takes the items of an iterator instance's Python __next__ without nesting on the C stack
- dict(), dict.update() and ** call the keys function an instance holds without nesting on the C stack
- sorted() and list.sort() call a key's reflected method after its NotImplemented without nesting on the C stack
- a dict subclass's __missing__ runs without nesting on the C stack for a tuple key that holds no instance
- print() reads its file's write and flush through a Python getter or __getattr__ without nesting on the C stack
- zip() and map() resume a generator after a str, bytes or dict iterable without nesting on the C stack
- ** of a dict or frozendict subclass that keeps dict iteration merges its own entries, as dict() does,
  without calling its keys or __getitem__
- a call's ** of a mapping with Python keys and __getitem__ runs without nesting on the C stack
- a call's ** reports a repeated keyword before reading its value, and a non-str key after every ** merges
- a stored dict or set key's Python __eq__ runs without nesting on the C stack when a builtin key is looked up, stored or deleted
- filter() of a Python function resumes its generator without nesting on the C stack
- a dropped generator in a yield from of a builtin iterator, or of an instance with a Python close,
  closes without nesting on the C stack
- a set's union(), update(), difference() and symmetric_difference_update() of a dict take its
  keys' stored hashes, as CPython does, and with intersection() and the rest do not nest on the C stack
- calling a set, frozenset, list, tuple or dict subclass of no __new__ or __init__ on a
  generator, or on items with a Python __hash__ or __eq__, does not nest on the C stack
- dict(), **, update(), copy(), | and a dict subclass merge a dict into an empty one comparing no keys
- a set's union(), intersection(), difference() and their updates of several sets, and a
  difference update by a much larger set, call a Python __eq__ without nesting on the C stack
- a set subclass's instance takes its set's operators, in-place operators and comparisons without
  nesting on the C stack; a comparison with a builtin subclass's instance tries its reflected one first
- a set's symmetric_difference(), symmetric_difference_update() and issubset() of an iterable
  call a Python __hash__ and __eq__ without nesting on the C stack
- a set's operator, in-place operator and comparison methods called by name, such as
  a.__ior__(b), call a Python __eq__ without nesting on the C stack
- set operations between two sets compare elements in CPython's order: intersection and
  isdisjoint walk the smaller set, and difference and symmetric difference look elements up again
- isinstance() and issubclass() of objects that are not classes read their Python __bases__ and
  __class__ without nesting on the C stack
- a set's union(), intersection(), difference(), symmetric_difference(), their updates, issubset(),
  issuperset() and isdisjoint() of another set call a Python __eq__ without nesting on the C stack
- set(s), frozenset(s), {*s} and the like of a set s into an empty set no longer call __eq__
- raise of an exception class, or from a cause class, calls its Python __init__ without nesting
  on the C stack, so it can pause
- a generator's throw() of an exception class, also with a value, calls its Python __init__
  without nesting on the C stack
- truth tests of a builtin subclass's instance, or of a class by its metaclass, call a Python
  __bool__ or __len__ without nesting on the C stack
- the truth of an int, float or complex subclass with a Python __len__ but no __bool__ is the number's
- not of a value whose __bool__ or __len__ fails no longer passes True to a call around it
- operators, comparisons and in of a builtin subclass's instance, or of a class by its metaclass,
  call its Python methods without nesting on the C stack
- the repr of a container calls its such items' Python __repr__ without nesting on the C stack
- isinstance() and issubclass() of a tuple call its classes' Python __instancecheck__ or
  __subclasscheck__ without nesting on the C stack
- a slice's Python __index__ parts in a subscript run without nesting on the C stack
- a subscript by a slice and slice.indices() check each part as they reach it, after the
  __index__ of the parts before it
- a list item store or deletion by an index with a Python __index__ runs it without nesting on the
  C stack
- a sequence repeated by an instance with a Python __index__ runs it without nesting on the C stack
- an item's index, a repetition's count or a bytes() size that cannot fit an index names the
  instance's type
- hex(), oct(), bin() and chr() of an instance call its Python __index__ without nesting on the
  C stack
- range() of instances calls their Python __index__ in turn without nesting on the C stack
- a list's pop() and insert() of an instance call its Python __index__ without nesting on the C stack
- bytes() and bytearray() of an instance call its Python __index__ without nesting on the C stack
- a builtin type's subclass without an operator method uses the builtin's in its turn; one
  defining the method gets no builtin result when it declines, but sequence concatenation stays
- an augmented assignment whose Python in-place method declines calls the plain operator's
  Python methods without nesting on the C stack
- a set's union(), intersection(), difference(), their updates, isdisjoint() and issuperset() of
  an iterable take and compare its items in turn, without nesting on the C stack
- a class with a Python __new__ called with * or ** arguments runs __new__ and __init__ without
  nesting on the C stack, so it can pause
- str(), repr(), format(), len(), hash(), int(), round() and the like call the Python method of a
  builtin type's subclass, or of a class's metaclass, without nesting on the C stack
- an iterator over a sequence whose __getitem__ raises an error other than IndexError or
  StopIteration tries the same index again on its next step
- dict.get(), setdefault() and pop() and set.add(), discard() and remove() call the Python
  __eq__ of a key of the same hash without nesting on the C stack, so they can pause
- a tuple key of a dict or set calls its items' Python __hash__ without nesting on the C stack,
  so it can pause
- hash() of a tuple, also as map()'s function, calls its items' Python __hash__ without nesting on
  the C stack, so it can pause
- set(), frozenset(), set.update() and dict.fromkeys() of tuples call their items' Python __hash__
  without nesting on the C stack, so they can pause
- a dict or set lookup of a tuple key, and set(), frozenset(), set.update() and dict.fromkeys() of
  tuples, call the Python __eq__ of the tuple's items and a stored tuple's without nesting on the C stack
- added spy_callk(): a host function calls Python as Lua's lua_callk does,
  with a continuation, so the call can pause and does not nest on the C stack
- make test-gc-stress runs the specification suite with frequent automatic collections

2026-11-10 0.9.0:

- Python code no longer runs on the C stack: calls, generators, coroutines,
  comprehensions, class statements, imports, exec() and eval(), and the
  special methods that operators and builtins call; recursion is bounded by
  the recursion limit alone
- added SPY_LIMIT_PAUSE, spy_paused() and spy_resume(): a spent budget or an
  interrupt pauses a call, which spy_resume() continues
- generators, coroutines and their throw() and close() follow CPython in
  more cases; a generator held by its own frame is collected
- yield from, await and raising an exception run about a third faster

2026-11-05 0.8.0:

- incompatible C API changes, the last planned; from 0.8.0 to 1.0.0 the
  API and ABI stay compatible:
  - spy_type() reports only the builtin data types and SPY_OBJECT
  - spy_set_step_limit() and spy_set_memory_limit() removed; use
    spy_set_limit() (also Go's SetStepLimit, Rust's set_step_limit)
- JavaScript package: no SharedArrayBuffer needed where JSPI exists;
  worker: false runs the engine on the calling thread
- spyc links libNAME.a archive modules
- spyc programs mount their directory, get sys.argv and exit as spy does
- added spy_set_argv(); spy passes its arguments into sys.argv
- == on deeply nested containers no longer raises RecursionError
- exception classes have CPython's own __str__, __repr__ and __new__

2026-11-05 0.7.0:

- the cycle collector runs automatically (SPY_LIMIT_COLLECTION; zero
  leaves it to spy_collect) and is about twice as fast on a large heap
- removed the gc module
- sorting is 3.6x faster (sort benchmark 128 ms -> 35 ms)
- tuples store their items inline
- Go, Rust, Java and the worker protocol load native modules

2026-11-03 0.6.0:

- incompatible C API changes, the last before 1.0:
  - spy_truth() takes the context and returns -1 when __bool__ or
    __len__ fails; Go's Value.Truth and Rust's Value::truth return the
    error
  - spy_set_output() and spy_set_error_output() take the stream's flush
    callback; spy_set_flush() is removed, and sys.stderr.flush() reaches
    the error stream's flush
  - SPYModuleLoader and SPYReadLine receive the context and return owned
    values (bytes source, str line) instead of malloc buffers, so host
    and engine never share an allocator
  - SPYWrite and SPYFlush receive the context first, as every callback
    but the interrupt and release do; a failing one may set its own error
- the stability promise covers spy.h; the contents of _spy are outside it

2026-11-03 0.5.0:

- step budgets, memory limits and an interrupt callback; the C API and
  packages report the steps a call spent
- every engine line runs under the tests; CI enforces coverage
- every C API declaration documented, ABI compatibility checked
- CPython 3.15.2 is the oracle; 26 more CPython language tests ported
  (72 in all)
- error messages, argument errors and keyword suggestions follow CPython
- str, bytes, tuple and dict subclasses accepted where builtins expect
  their base type
- spy, spyc and spy-worker binaries published per release tag
- faster than CPython 3.15 on loop, bigint, dicts, strings and classes,
  within 4% on sort, nbody and generators
- embedding lifecycle in 186 us (was 192 us)

2026-10-31 0.4.0:

- faster than CPython 3.15 on loop, bigint, dicts and classes, within 13%
  on the other benchmark programs
- values allocated from pools in three size classes
- small numbers and short str and bytes stored inline
- reused function environments and cached name lookups
- generator bodies run more of their work as machine instructions
- embedding lifecycle in 192 us (was 257 us)

2026-10-31 0.3.0:

- added Rust crates and a Go module
- spyc: bundle imported modules into the C output (-m, -I, -x, --alias,
  --metafile, --deps, --analyze, --minify, ...)
- added spy_set_optimize(); spy: added -O and -OO
- sys and gc follow CPython 3.15 (settrace, setprofile, excepthook,
  unraisablehook, lazy imports, gc counts and freezing)
- added spy_set_error_output()
- removed the weakref module
- added allocation failure, ThreadSanitizer and coverage tests, and a
  spyc fuzzer
- added the flappy, pong and todo examples
- Cython baseline: 383 static and 407 dynamic examples pass

2026-10-29 0.2.0:

- faster name lookup, builtin dispatch and attribute access
- faster dicts, sets, list copies, sort and non-ASCII strings
- Karatsuba multiplication and Knuth division for big integers
- 'x in bytes' accepts any bytes-like needle
- Unicode tables in the libunicode encoding (732 KB to 343 KB)

2026-10-26 0.1.0:

- first release: C11 interpreter with the spy_* embedding API
- reference counting and a cycle collector
- spy interpreter and spyc C source generator
- arbitrary precision integers, complex numbers, exact float rounding
- classes, C3 MRO, super, metaclasses, descriptors, __slots__
- generators, coroutines, async generators and comprehensions
- pattern matching, f-strings, template strings, PEP 563 and PEP 649
  annotations, lazy imports (PEP 810)
- frozendict, memoryview and the buffer protocol (PEP 688)
- %-formatting, str.format and the format mini-language ported from CPython
- Unicode 17 identifiers, names, properties and NFKC normalization
- PEP 263 source encodings
- package, relative and namespace package imports, import hooks
- open() restricted to directories mounted with spy_mount()
- CPython 3.15 error messages and source ranges
- standard modules moved out of the core (spy_new_module()); the
  standard layer spy-std.c supplies sys and gc
- added spy_set_opaque(), spy_set_input() and spy_collect()
- added the small-python Python package
- PLS-315, the language standard, with spec tests and TLA+ models for
  every clause
- ported CPython language tests in tests/spec/ports
- AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer runs
